stepping:
Across devouring waters, followed her,
Stepping by her footsteps, breathing by her breath,
And holding her with eyes that would not slip;

step:
An' I feel my feddahs rufflin' all de day,
An' my laigs commence to trimble evah blessid step I mek;
W'en I sees a ax, I tu'ns my head away.

steals:
Ah, Nora, my Nora, the light fades away,
While Night like a spirit steals up o'er the hills;
The thrush from his tree where he chanted all day,

steely:
The women thronged to look, but never a one
Showed sorrow in her eyes of steely blue;
And little lads, lynchers that were to be,

stead:
I, who have given so much, and asked so little.
Some day, when there's another in my stead;
Again you'll feel the need of absolution,

sterile:
Its other realm of sea;
Without the sterile perquisite
No Caspian could be.

stewing:
Monty too;
The little portress at the Convent School, stewing in hell
so patiently;

steppin:
Feel lak stone;
Easy steppin', blessid feet,
Small an' sweet.

steeple:
Slow and slow the great bell swung,
It hung in the steeple mute;
And people tore its living tongue

steed:
But, “Even Stephen,” he cried, and struck
His steed an urgent blow;
He swore by youth he was a buck

steamin:
We had wheat bread white ez cotton an' a egg pone jes like gol',
Hog jole, bilin' hot an' steamin' roasted shoat an' ham sliced cold--
Look out! What's de mattah wif you? Don't be fallin' on de flo';

stealin:
Wintah time hit comin'
Stealin' thoo de night;
Wake up in the mo'nin'

steppe:
Were over, and the morning was in sight--
I cried, but in strange places, steppe and firth
I have not seen, through alien grief and mirth;

stealth's:
Day rattles, too,
Stealth's slow;
The sun has got as far

steadier:
A bride-chant burdened with one name;
The bride-song rises steadier
Than the torches' flame:

stedfast:
From their calm locks, and undiscomfited
Look stedfast truths against Time’s changing mask.
True, many a prophet teaches in the roads;
